[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the units digit of the sum, \( 1! + 2! + 3! + \cdots + 2006! \), we need to determine the units digit of each factorial and then sum them up for \( n \) factorial.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, let's observe the pattern in the units digits of the first few factorials:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 1! = 1 \) (units digit: \( 1 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 2! = 2 \) (units digit: \( 2 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 3! = 6 \) (units digit: \( 6 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 4! = 24 \) (units digit: \( 4 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 5! = 120 \) (units digit: \( 0 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 6! = 720 \) (units digit: \( 0 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 7! = 5040 \) (units digit: \( 0 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 8! = 40320 \) (units digit: \( 0 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We can see that the units digit repeats every 4 factors. To confirm, let's check the units digits of the next few factorial numbers:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 5! = 120 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 6! = 720 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 7! = 5040 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 8! = 40320 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The units digit of \( 8! = 40320 \) is \( 0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, the units digit of the sum \( 1! + 2! + 3! + 4! + 5! + \cdots + 2006! \) will be the same as the units digit of \( 1! + 2! + 3! + 4! + 0! + 5! + 6! + 7! + \cdots + 2006! \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since \( 0! = 1 \), the units digit will be the same as the units digit of \( 1! + 2! + 3! + 4! + 0! + 5! + 6! + 7! + \cdots + 2006! \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since \( 1! = 1 \), \( 2! = 2 \), \( 3! = 6 \), \( 4! = 24 \), \( 0! = 1 \), \( 5! = 120 \), \( 6! = 720 \), \( 7! = 5040 \), \( 8! = 40320 \), and so on, the units digit will be determined by the units digits of the factorials from 1 to 4.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's calculate the units digit of the sum:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 1! = 1 \) (units digit: \( 1 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 2! = 2 \) (units digit: \( 2 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 3! = 6 \) (units digit: \( 6 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( 4! = 24 \) (units digit: \( 4 \))
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Adding these together: \( 1 + 2 + 6 + 4 = 13 \). The units digit of 13 is \( 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, the units digit of the sum \( 1! + 2! + 3! + \cdots + 2006! \) is \(\boxed{3}\).<|endoftext|>
