[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The polynomials $(x - 2)^4 - (x - 2) = 0$ and $x^2 - kx + k = 0$ have two roots in common.  Find the value of $k.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, note that both polynomials share a common root, $(x-2)$, which implies that $x-2-1 = (x-2)(x-1-1^1) = 0$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let \((x-2)[(x-1) + 1] = (2x-3)(x-1) + 2$. This simplifies to $2x-3 = x^2 - x - 3$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Rewriting an expression, we find \((x-3)\). Additionally, $$(x-2)(x-1-1) = (x-3)(x-2).$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the common root is also 3.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Substituting, we see that the quadratic equation $x^2 - kx + k = 0$ describes $3^2 - 3k + k = 0$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Rewriting the expression, we find $k = 3^2 - 3 \cdot 3 + 3 = 3.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m enity<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m False
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
