[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To determine how many positive integers \( a_1 \leq 2008 \) satisfy the given conditions, we need to analyze the recursive definition \( a_n = \frac{a_{n-1}}{2} \) if \( a_{n-1} \) is even and \( a_n = 3a_{n-1} + 1 \) if \( a_{n-1} \) is odd.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### Step 1: Understanding the Recurrence Relations
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - For even \( a_{n-1} \), we have \( a_n = \frac{a_{n-1}}{2} \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - For odd \( a_{n-1} \), we have \( a_n = 3a_{n-1} + 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### Step 2: Initial Values
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We need to determine the initial values of \( a_n \). To do this, we examine the first few terms:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - For even \( a_1 \), the sequence will be \( a_1, \frac{a_1}{2}, a_1, \frac{a_1}{2}, a_1, \ldots \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - For odd \( a_1 \), the sequence will be \( a_1, 3a_1 + 1, a_1, 3a_1 + 1, a_1, \ldots \).
