[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To determine the 42nd card in Emily's pile of playing cards, we need to consider the cyclical nature of the number of suits (Ace, 2, ..., 10, 11, 12, Jack, Queen, King) within each suit. There are 13 suits in total.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. **Sum of Suits**: Each suit has 13 cards. Therefore, together they have $13$ suits. This means each suit contributes $13$ cards to the counting process.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. **Number of Cards**: Since there are 13 cards per suit and a total of 42 cards, the sequence of houses on the 42nd position will be generated by an increase of $12$ sections (loosing $13$ cards) of the 13 suits.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. **Sequence Calculation**:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - At the $12$th section (loosing card from $3$ suits), there are $42 -12 = 21$ cards left.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - At the $13$th section (another unborrowed card from $3$ suits), there are $21 -13 = 8$ cards left.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - At the $14$th section (another unborrowed card from $3$ suits), there are $8 -13 = -5$ cards left, indicating missing one of the remaining cards. This is consistent with having $12$ cards left in the first place.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Keeping in mind we should never run out of cards (i.e., $13$ cards per section), it follows that after filling out the $13$ sections we leave $8$ unused cards, not filled, leaving us with $6$ cards.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the $42$nd card (order read as the 6th card out of the series) would indeed be the 6th card, which corresponds to the Ace of Hearts.
