[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the roots of the equation \(x^3 - 3x^2 - 10x + 24 = 0\), we will use a combination of methods, including factoring, synthetic division, and numerical methods. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We will start by trying to factor the polynomial using classical factorings that we know. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Using synthetic division, we will divide \(x^3 - 3x^2 - 10x + 24\) by \(x - 4\), which is a root of the polynomial.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Failing this, we can also use a substitution approach to reach the roots more directly.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Next:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. \(x = 4\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[4^3 - 3(4^2) - 10(4) + 24 = 64 - 48 - 40 + 24 = 8 - 40 + 24 = -28 + 24 = -4\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, \(4\) is a root.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Now we divide \(x^3 - 3x^2 - 10x + 24\) by \(x - 4\) using synthetic division:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m  \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{array}{r|rrrr}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m  4 & 1 & -3 & -10 & 24 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m  0 & 1 & 7  & 28 & -96 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m  1 & 1 & 4  & 18 & 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   This gives:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m  $$1 \cdot (x - 4) + 1 \cdot 14 - 7 \cdot x + 28 - 96 = x - 4$$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m  
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So the reduced equation is: 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[x^2 + 14x - 92 = 0\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
