[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the greatest two-digit whole number where the product of its digits is equal to 8, we must identify a number whose digits multiply to give 8. The largest possible two-digit number with this property is 94, because 9 × 4 = 36. By increasing the first digit to 9, we can multiply by 8, resulting in 9 × 8 = 72. However, 94 is not the largest possible value since it has digits greater than 9 and 0, which no longer satisfy the condition. The next larger integer is 98, giving a product of 8 × 9 = 72, and when we alter the first digit to 8, we get a product of 8 × 8 = 64, which is smaller than 72. Therefore, 98 is the greatest two-digit multiple of 8 where the product of the digits is 8.<|endoftext|>
