[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the sum of the final three digits of the integer representation of $5^{100}$, we first need to determine the value of $5^{100}$. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $5^{100}$ is a very large number, with the thousands and hundreds digits relocating as $5$. To find the last three digits of this number, we compute $5$ raised to the power of $100$, and consider the remaining part of the last three digits. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We start computation with $5^{100}$. We can observe that:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$5^0 = 1$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$5^1 = 5$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$5^2 = 25$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m On checking multiples of powers of $5$, we notice that the remaining digits of the final three digits, if we disregard the trailing zeros from subsequent computation reducing the exponents quickly, are the digits which remain when $5^i$ is divided by $100$, whose actual value (use $5 \cdot 0 \cdot 0$ in division) can be found out by evaluating $5^i|0 < \text{div} performed by division|0$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Next, the first three digits arising from the usual integer division method will be obtained by evaluating $5^{100|0 < number>0}$, where 'multiplier' of the quotient will be also $0$, the final three digits of the number generated will be.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore the answer is $5\cdot0=.<|endoftext|>
