[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m How many different prime factors does $20!$ have? (Reminder: If $n$ is a positive integer, then $n!$ stands for the product $1\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot \cdots \cdot (n-1)\cdot n$.)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, we need to find the prime factorization of $20!$. Since $20! = 2^a \cdot 3^b \cdot 5^c \cdots 20^d$, where $a$, $b$, $c$, $\ldots$, $d$ are non-negative integers, we can rewrite this as:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$20! = 2^a \cdot 3^b \cdot 5^c \cdots 20^d$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now we will find the unique highest powers of each prime factor we can have in this number.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $2$, since $20$ is divisible by $2$ ($20/2 = 10$), any power of $2$ in $20!$ will be at most $5$ (since $2 \times 10$ is divisible by $10$ and any higher power of $2$ would be just less than $10$).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $3$, since $20$ is not divisible by $3$, there is no power of $3$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $5$, since $20$ is divisible by $5$, a power of $5$ in $20!$ will be at most $2$ (since $2\cdot 10$ is divisible by $10$).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $7$, since $20$ is not divisible by $7$, there is no power of $7$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $11$, since $20$ is not divisible by $11$, there is no power of $11$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $13$, since $20$ is not divisible by $13$, there is no power of $13$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $17$, since $20$ is not divisible by $17$, there is no power of $17$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $19$, since $20$ is not divisible by $19$, there is no power of $19$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m * For $23$, since $20$ is not divisible by $23$, there is no power of $23$ in $20!$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, out of the existing prime factors, we have the following unique highest powers of $2$ up to $2$ up to $\boxed{2} \in \mathbb{N}$, $20! = 2^a \cdot 3^b \cdot 5^c \cdots 20^d$.
