[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m .“When the innocent are present,what great notions must the guilty have? “
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m While many hold that criminal justice is a function of instinct, arguing that guilt is not intrinsic to the human mind, Dr. J.T. Jensen, CLC Ph.D., the chair at the Justice Center in Iowa City, soon challenged that logic.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Jensen says being inquisitive and asking questions contributes much to enhancing the criminal justice system’s purposes and has been a focal point in his own studies on the topic.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m “Juror’s tend to be ethically aware and, therefore, function better as a judge and dispatcher, and they do serve up to target,” Jensen said.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Jensen discovered that as other factors are removed that jurors tend to forming better judgments and develop a better understanding of the potential impact their disciplinary actions have on the community.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m However, according to the release Jensen’s original work found that the most devastating impact from the judge is by making a mistake. “According to modern psychological theories, the disrespectful attitude that the jury have toward the defendant related to wrongful conviction can only accelerate criminalization. The problem with that is, it invalidates the administration of justice, thus, we have no accountability of jurors,” he said.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Recently, Jensen authored a presentation for the American Bar Association Law Journal that highlighted three reasons jurors and jurors’ divisions strengthen the individual justice system’s functions.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m “So it is how the jury engages its judges, judges and their determination structures that are more reflective of the way reality intervenes into justice — the governing body of the process intertwines its nous with psych equal voices delineate its objectives — regardless of what they advise and decide,” Jensen said in the CLC release.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Clauses 3 and 4 suggest that state attorneys, who should scrutinize their jurors’ issues from state anti-offense documents, are capable of addressing those catalysts when they appear on the docket.
