[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since \( w = z^4 \), the possible values for \( w \) are \( 1 \) and \( (-7 + 8\sqrt{3}) \), which means \( z^4 = 1 \) or \( z^4 = -7 + 8\sqrt{3} \). We have \( z^4 = 1 \) with solutions \( z = 1 \) and \( z = -1 \), and \( z^4 = -7 + 8\sqrt{3} \) with solutions \( z = \sqrt[4]{-7 + 8\sqrt{3}} = \sqrt{I} \) (the principal fourth root of \(-7 + 8\sqrt{3}\)) and \( z = -\sqrt[4]{i\sqrt{14} + 7} = -\sqrt{-1 \cdot (4\sqrt{3} + 7)} = -\sqrt{\overline{(-7 + 8\sqrt{3})}} = -\sqrt{-1 \cdot (-7 + 8\sqrt{3})} = -\sqrt{7 - 8\sqrt{3}} = -\sqrt{\overline{8\sqrt{3} - 7}} = -\sqrt{8\sqrt{3} - 7} \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The fourth roots of \(-7 + 8\sqrt{3}\) are \( \sqrt{14}^{1/4} \), \( -\sqrt{14}^{1/4} \), \( -\sqrt{216}^{1/4} \), and \( \sqrt{216}^{1/4} \), which are \( \frac{\sqrt[4]{14}}{2} = \frac{7}{2\sqrt{66}} \), \( -\frac{7}{2\sqrt{66}} \), \( -\frac{9}{2\sqrt{66}} \), and \( \frac{7}{2\sqrt{66}} \).
