[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Yes, that's correct. To determine how many six-inch by six-inch square tiles are needed to cover a three-foot by two-foot rectangular section of floor, let's follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Convert the dimensions from feet to inches:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - 3 feet = 3 × 12 = 36 inches
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - 2 feet = 2 × 12 = 24 inches
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Calculate the area of the floor:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \text{Area of the floor} = \text{Length} \times \text{Width} = 36 \, \text{inches} \times 24 \, \text{inches} = 864 \, \text{square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. Calculate the area of one tile:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \text{Area of one tile} = \text{Sides} \times \text{Sides} = 6 \times 6 \, \text{square inches} = 36 \, \text{square inches}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 4. Calculate the number of tiles needed by dividing the area of the floor by the area of one tile:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \text{Number of tiles} = \frac{\text{Area of the floor}}{\text{Area of one tile}} = \frac{864 \, \text{square inches}}{36 \, \text{square inches}} = 24
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, you need \boxed{24} six-inch by six-inch square tiles to cover the three-foot by two-foot rectangular section of floor.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m [Ground Truth]
