[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Compute $\sin 180^\circ$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To compute \(\sin 180^\circ\), we can use the sine function in trigonometry, which relates the angles in a unit circle to the coordinates of the endpoints of a semicircle.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In a unit circle, an angle of \(180^\circ\) indicates that the terminal side of the angle lies on the positive x-axis. The sine of an angle in a unit circle is the y-coordinate of the point on the circumference corresponding to that angle.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For an angle of \(180^\circ\), the point on the circumference corresponds to the coordinates \((0, 0)\). Therefore, the y-coordinate (which is the sine of the angle) is \(0\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, \(\sin 180^\circ = 0\).
