[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To answer the question, we need to identify prime numbers whose squares lie between 100 and 300.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 1: List the numbers whose squares lie between 100 and 300
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The smallest square greater than 100 is 144 (12^2), and the largest square smaller than 300 is 2\*225 (15*15).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 225/144 ≈ 1.64...
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, "greater than" 100 and "less than" 300 indicate that we are looking at numbers like 144, which are prime(not divisible by 2, other than 1 and it itself).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 2: Identify the prime numbers which lie between 144 and 225 to form squares.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We start checking 144 -> 56, 64, 118... as 64 is also missing in our completed list.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 3: Find all prime numbers which meet the criteria.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m From the list of prime numbers:,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Prime numbers meeting the criteria 144 -> 56, 64, 218, 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m prime numbers meeting the criteria 144 -> 56, 64, 118
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ...
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We can check from 64 to 375.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m However, after a few checks, we reach the point where we no longer need to check for 375 when looking for the sqrt of any value. We either must skip adjacent values or find a value to use for further the going.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Okay, so we can remove 64 and join with 118 which already leaves us 144 and hence 56 and 64.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
