[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To solve this problem, we must first understand the definition of diagonals in a convex heptagon.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m A diagonal in a convex polygon is a line segment connecting two non-adjacent vertices.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In a Heptagon, adding the diagonals would involve adding all possible pairs of non-adjacent vertices.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's compute the number of these pairs:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. skipping the first and last vertex pair 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. skipping all the other edge pairs (excluding the first and last)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Hence, going back to the steps above, we have 6 sides of the diagonal.
