[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Of course! Here's the solution step-by-step:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The matrix \( A = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \) must be its own inverse. This means that \( A \) and \( A^{-1} \) are equal, meaning they produce the same vector when applied to one another. That is, \( A \cdot A^{-1} = I \), where \( I \) is the identity matrix.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find \( c \) and \( d \), let's compute both sides of the equation step-by-step:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Compute \( A \cdot A^{-1} \):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m A \cdot A^{-1} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \cdot 3 + (-1) \cdot c & 3 \cdot (-1) + (-1) \cdot d \\ c \cdot 3 + d \cdot c & c \cdot (-1) + d \cdot d \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Given that \( A \) and \( A^{-1} \) are equal, they must be symmetric (since they're inverses of a matrix):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 3 & c \\ c & d \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\ c & d \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m verification: 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 3 \cdot 3 + c \cdot c & 3 \cdot (-1) + c \cdot d \\ c \cdot 3 + d \cdot c & c \cdot (-1) + d \cdot d \end{pmatrix} = 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 9 + c^2 & -3 + cd \\ 3c + dc & -c + d^2 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since both of these must have invertible form, from symmetry, both had to be matrix equations:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
