[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.\]Compute $\mathbf{A}^{95}.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To compute $\mathbf{A}^{95}$, we can use the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix $\mathbf{A}$. First, we need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\mathbf{A}$. We can do this by solving the characteristic equation $\det(\mathbf{A} - \lambda \mathbf{I}) = 0$. The eigenvalues will be the solutions of this equation.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The matrix is:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The characteristic equation is:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \det(\mathbf{A} - \lambda \mathbf{I}) = \det \begin{pmatrix} -\lambda & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & -\lambda & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & -\lambda \end{pmatrix} = \lambda^3 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the eigenvalues are $\lambda_1 = 0$, $\lambda_2 = 0$, and $\lambda_3 = 3$. Now we find the eigenvectors corresponding to each eigenvalue.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For $\lambda_1 = 0$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This gives us the equation $x_3 = 0$. So, an eigenvector is $\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For $\lambda_2 = 0$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} -\lambda_1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & -\lambda_1 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & -\lambda_1 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This gives us the equation $x_3 = 0$. So, an eigenvector is $\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For $\lambda_3 = 3$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This gives us the equation $-3x_3 = 0$, so $x_3 = 0$. So, an eigenvector is $\mathbf{v}_3 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Next, we form the matrix $\mathbf{P}$ whose columns are the eigenvectors $\mathbf{v}_1$, $\mathbf{v}_2$, and $\mathbf{v}_3$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \mathbf{P} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The matrix $\mathbf{P}$ is orthonormal, and we can compute $\mathbf{A}^{95}$ by raising $\mathbf{A}$ to the power of 95 and then using the matrix $\mathbf{P}$ to get back to $\mathbf{A}$. We can do this using the exponential and power series methods.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, we find the exponential of $\mathbf{A}$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \mathbf{P}_0 \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}_0^{-1} = \text{exp}(95 \cdot 0) \mathbf{A} = \text{identity matrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This tells us that $\mathbf{A}^{95} = \mathbf{P}_0 \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}_0^{-1} \mathbf{A}^n \mathbf{P}_1^t = \mathbf{A}^n$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m where $\mathbf{P}_0$ and $\mathbf{P}_1$ are obtained by conjugating $\mathbf{P}$. We already have $\mathbf{P}_0 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$, and $\mathbf{P}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$. So, we need to raise this to the power of 95:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \mathbf{A}^{95} = \mathbf{A}^{95} = \mathbf{A}^{\frac{95}{3}} = \mathbf{P}_0 \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}_0^{-1} \mathbf{A}^2 = \mathbf{P}_0 \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}_0^{-1} \mathbf{A}^{15} \mathbf{P}_1^t = \boxed{\mathbf{P}_0 \mathbf{A} \mathbf{P}_0^{-1} \mathbf{A}^t}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Finally, to get $\mathbf{A}^{15}$, we can use the fact that $\mathbf{P} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ and multiply $\mathbf{A}$ by $\mathbf{P}^2$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \mathbf{A}^{95} = \mathbf{P}_1 \mathbf{A}^2 \mathbf{P}_1^t = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So the final answer is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}}$.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & -1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m [Is Boxed Matched]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m True
