[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To show that the matrix \[\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{pmatrix}\] is not invertible, we need to find a non-trivial solution \( \mathbf{x} \) such that \( \mathbf{x} \mathbf{A} = \mathbf{0} \), where \( \mathbf{A} \) is the matrix.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's assume the matrix multiplication results in the zero vector:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\begin{pmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{pmatrix} \mathbf{x} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This gives us the system of equations:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ax + by + cz = 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m bx + cy + az = 0 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m cx + ay + bz = 0
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This setup forms a homogeneous system of linear equations. If we multiply the first equation by \( a \), the second equation by \( b \), and the third equation by \( c \), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
