[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the least common multiple (LCM) of 14 and 21, we need to first determine the prime factors of each number.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 14 can be factored into 2 × 7 (which includes 2 and 7).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 21 can be factored into 3 × 7 (which includes 3 and 7).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, we multiply all the prime factors with their respective highest power to find the LCM.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m LCM(14, 21) = 2 × 7 × 3 × 7
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m LCM(14, 21) = 2 × 7^2 × 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m LCM(14, 21) = 2 × 49 × 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m LCM(14, 21) = 98 × 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m LCM(14, 21) = 294
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, the least common multiple of 14 and 21 is 294.
