[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m It's didn't make any sense.gressive hexagon has always been:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Although this information appears a bit unusual at first glance, it actually has important implications for the calculation of the area of a regular hexagon.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The key aspect of the problem is that inscribed hexagon is a regular hexagon, meaning all its sides and internal angles match those of its circumscribed hexagon.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Imagine dividing the circle into two equal parts and taking a hexagon inside the circle. Each of these halves will be a part of the circumscribed hexagon.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, arc lengths of line segments intersecting inside a hexagon are useful for finding the area. Just the beauty of the hexagon - being a hex, naturally allows this to be done in a systematic way.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Note that the inscribed hexagon can be seen as a centrosymmetric pair of triangles. The center of the circle can be considered as the center of one triangle, and its opposite center (in the plane of the hexagon so the circle) as the other triangle.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This provides two equilateral triangles, with each having an equilateral angle of \(\frac{2 \pi}{6} = \frac{\pi}{3} \text{ radians}\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Each triangular area is therefore of \(\frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times 2 = \sqrt{3}\) square units.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The three innermost \(\frac{\pi}{3}\) triangle areas form the area we seek.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m combe set:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First we note that the hexagon traced and projected onto the circle, is actually a pair of two equilateral triangles. Each with an area of \(\frac{1}{2}\ π = \frac{\pi}{2}\ ie. in square inches. covered over this area<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
