[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If $\log (xy^3)= 1$ and $\log (x^2y) = 1$,  what is $\log (xy)$?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 1:  Given the equations with the log terms, we can establish a relationship between the variables. This involves understanding how logs interact when applied to products and powers of a common base.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 2:  We have two equations:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m       \log (xy^3) &=1\\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m       \log (x^2y) &=1
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   Since the base is also provided as 10 by the problem, we can simplify our next action. for these logarithms they are easier to compare in some way, use the rule log$_10(x/N)$/ log $_010$\n\n
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   Now we"d have  $\log xy=1 $( 1) & $\log (x^2y)=1 (2)$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 3:  Remove the logs on both sides of the equation (1) and 2).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   $\log xy=1 \rightarrow xy=10^1 \rightarrow x=\frac{10^2}{10} \rightarrow x=10$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   $\log (x^2y)=1 \rightarrow x^2y=10^1 \rightarrow x^2y=10$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 4:  Using these solved values, we can determine our $\logxy$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   Y=$\frac{1}{10^2} = \frac{1}{100}$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m   Therefore, $ \log xy=\boxed{\frac{1}{100}}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
