[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In an acute triangle \(ABC\), the angle bisectors \(ACB\) and \(BCA\) intersect at \(I\), the incenter. The sides opposite to the points of intersection are \(AB\), \(AC\), and \(BC\). Let \(O\) be the center of the inscribed circle, and consider the points \(D\) and \(E\) on \(AI\) and \(AC\) such that \(DO \perp AB\) and \(EO \perp AC\). Find the angle \(BOE\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Given an acute triangle \(ABC\) with incenter \(I\), the angles at vertices \(A\) and \(B\) are \(\angle BAC\) and \(\angle ABC\) respectively, and \(BAC = \alpha\). The side opposite to \(I\) is \(BC\), and its angle bisector intersects \(BC\) at \(D\). The points of intersection of the angle bisectors \(ACB\) and \(BCA\) are \(I\) and \(E\) respectively. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We need to find the angle \(\angle BOE\). First, we note that since \([ABC]\) is a triangle, \(\angle BAC = \alpha\), and the angle bisectors intersect at \(I\), which is the incenter. The incenter is the intersection of the angle bisectors, and for an acute triangle, it is the center of the circumcircle.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since \(I\) is the incenter, it lies on the angle bisector of \(\angle BAC\). This means that \(D\) is the point where the angle bisector \(ACB\) intersects the side \(BC\). Similarly, \(E\) lies on the angle bisector \(BCA\) and \(BC\) as well. The equidistant circles from \(O\) and \(I\) centered at \(B\) and \(C\) respectively form angles at \(B\) and \(C\) that are complementary to the angle \(\angle BIC = 90^\circ\) since \(I\) at the incenter of \(\triangle ABC\) is equidistant from the vertices \(B\) and \(C\) in terms of their distance from the circumcenter.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since \(AO = AI = IE = \text{inradius}\), \(\angle BOE\) is equal to \(\angle ODI\) which by the properties of a triangle, is the angle between the angle bisector \(CI\) and the line \(DO\). Given the symmetry from the angle bisector properties and the equidistant properties from the center of the circumcircle and the incenter, we know that \( \angle BOE = 90^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the angle \(\angle BOE\) is \(\boxed{90^\circ}\).<|endoftext|>
