[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m A standard deck of 52 cards has 13 ranks (Ace, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, Jack, Queen, King) and 4 suits ($\spadesuit$, $\heartsuit$, $\diamondsuit$, and $\clubsuit$), such that there is exactly one card for any given rank and suit.  Two of the suits ($\spadesuit$ and $\clubsuit$) are black and the other two suits ($\heartsuit$ and $\diamondsuit$) are red.  The deck is randomly arranged. What is the probability that the top card is the King of $\diamondsuit$?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Sure! The probability that the top card is the King of Hearts (King of Diamonds, or KxD, let's call it KxD) is 0/4=0.  The steps below provide the full analysis and rationale.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Calculate the total number of possible outcomes when randomly drawing a card.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    $\text{number of cards in the deck} = 52$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Calculate the number of favorable outcomes for getting the King of Hearts.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    The King of Hearts fits into the hands category (♣). There is only 1 choice for this rank in the deck ($♥$), hence $\text{number of favorable outcomes} = 1$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. Compute the probability as a quotient (if any) of favorable outcomes and total outcomes:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    $\text{probability} = \dfrac{\text{favorable outcome}}{\text{total outcomes}} = \dfrac{1}{52}$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 4. Elasticimize the probability to 0
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \boxed{0}<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m [Ground Truth]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \dfrac{1}{52}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
