[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 1: Determine the equation of the line with slope 3 that intersects the line with slope 5 at the point (10, 15). The equation of a line in slope-intercept form is y = mx + b. Given that it intersects at point (10, 15), we can plug in these values into the equation to find the y-intercept: 15 = 3(10) + b. Solving for b, we get b = -3.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 2: Determine the equation of the other line. In order to find the x-intercept when the other line intersects the y-axis, multiply b by the x-intercept for both lines. So the x-intercept of the other line would be 8 (i.e., x-intercept when m = -3/5). We can then find the y-intercept for the other line, substituting x-intercept 8 and original slope 5 into the equation: 5 = (-3/5)(8) + b.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 3: Solve for b to determine the y-intercept of the other line. 5 = -4.8 + b, so b = 9.8. Here, the y-intercept is 9.8. The other intercepts are 10 and 8.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 4: Calculate the distance between the two intercepts: distance = sqrt[ p(10) + q(8) ]^2 + (p(-3) + q(-3) )^2 ^2, where p and q represent the intercepts you just determined.
