[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The given problem asks to compute $\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{7}\right)\tan\left(\frac{2\pi}{7}\right)\tan\left(\frac{3\pi}{7}\right)$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, let's consider $r\in \mathbb{R}$, then we can rewrite our angle as $\arctan\left(\tan\left(\frac{\pi }{7}\right)-\tan\left(\frac{2\pi }{7}\right)\arctan\left(\tan\left(\frac{\pi }{7}\right)\right)\arctan\left(\tan\left(\frac{\pi }{7}\right)\right)\right).$ Further, we can proceed with $\theta=\arctan\left((\tan\left(\frac{\pi }{7}\right)-\tan\left(\frac{2\pi }{7}\right))\cot \left(\frac{\pi }{7}\right)\right).$ Thus, $\tan$ from these procedures might suggest we are on a path towards a telescoping series.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m However, there's a realized countable error there, which turns out to be $2 - \cot(\frac{\pi}{7}).$ Regarding minimal accuracy, we can look at the precision $\approx 0.1105413035$ and find that $\frac{\pi}{7}$ is the $\pi$ corresponding to that precision.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Actually, $\frac{\pi}{7}$ has been chiseled into desperate digits along with appropriate leading zeroes from time to time. The convergent interval of the series must potentially result in $\frac{\pi}{7}.$
