[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, we need to find what 35 problems represent by multiplying by the percentage score needed. That means:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ 35 \text{ problems} \times 0.80 = \text {$28$ problems} \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This means that 28 problems must be correct in order to pass the test (\(96\%\)) because 35 mistakes are needed to reach 90% after correcting 28 of them.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now let's consider the worst-case scenario where an exception occurs. Since you know \(90\%\), then missing 28 issues out of 35 directly gives you the highest but still invalid assessment.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the maximum number of mistakes you can make without getting the score of 80% on a 35-problem test (when each ideal issue requires an accurate answer with certainty) is simply the lower bound for the number of mistakes you miss: 28 bad moves!
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, the maximum number of problems you can miss without scoring a passing grade (80%) for this scenario is \(\boxed{28}\).<|endoftext|>
