[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If the consecutive integers from $50$ to $1$ were written as $$5049484746...,$$ what would be the $67^{\text{th}}$ digit to be written?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\left\{\begin{array}{@{}|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|} \begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\hline 50 & =400 \\ 41 & =120 \\ 42 & =250 \\ 43 & =350 \\ 44 & =060 \\ 45 & =300 \\ 46 & =450 \\ 47 & =560 \\ 48 & =600 \\ \ldots \\\hline\end{array}\right.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m PostBack
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m I'll break this down step by step:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 1: Identify the pattern
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m From the table above, we can see that the numbers are doubled and then summed: `41 + 120 + 250 + 350 + 060 + 120 = 1431`, then divided by 100 to get the next digit, and so on. It appears that each pair of digits repeats every 6 numbers. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 2: Determine the group
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m When we write out the first 120 six-digit numbers, we can group them into 20 sets of 6 digits each. This means that the first 56 numbers (50-106, 40-106) form a single group of numbers from 41 to 130, and the remaining 54 numbers (47-140, 48-140), will be part of a group of numbers from 141 to 220.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 3: Determine the position within the groups
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The group between 47-140 starts at the next digit after the digit 47 (which is actually 48), so it's the first half of the group from 47-140. The next number after it will be 49 (digit 50). The group between 141-220 starts at the next digit after the digit 141 (which is into the double 150 and thus how we see both groups in total within one group), so it's the first half from 141-220. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 4: Deduce the digit within the group
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m As we read each number in the groups, the numerals in the first half are continuously going into the same set of numbers:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ```
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m            Step: [47-140] [48-140] 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         First Half: [48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56] 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         = 48, 49, 50, 51, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         = Set A
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         = [9, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m           Step: [141-220] [141, 142] 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         Second Half: [141, 142] 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         = [99, 100] 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         = Set B
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m         = [99, 100] 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ```
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 5: Calculate the digit in the 67th position in each group
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since the last digit of the 67th number in the first half of the group is 8, it’s the 4th digit in the group A, and since the last digit of the 67th number in the second half (Before the double) is 1 (which we fill into the 1st half before repeating), the 67th digit will be in Set B (from 4 in the first half and 4 in the second half). 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Note: We are considering numbers starting from 141, so the last digit of the first number is not included.
