[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - \(M\) is the midpoint of \(OX\), so \(OM = \frac{OX}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - \(N\) is the midpoint of \(OY\), so \(ON = \frac{OY}{2}\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. **Form a Right-Angled Triangle**:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - In \(\triangle XOY\), \(XN\) and \(YM\) are the segments of \(OX\) and \(OY\) respectively.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Since \(XN = 19\) and \(YM = 22\), and \(M\) and \(N\) both lie on \(OY\), we need to find the length of \(OX\) and \(OY\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. **Apply the Pythagorean Theorem**:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Since \(XN\) and \(YM\) are the legs of the right triangle, and \(Y\) and \(O\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \(X\) and \(O\) to \(OY\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem in the triangle formed by \(OY\) and the segments of \(OX\) and \(OY\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Let \(OX = a\) and \(OY = b\). Then, \(XN = 19\) and \(YM = 22\), so \(XN^2 + ON^2 = XO^2\) and \(YM^2 + OM^2 = YO^2\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
