[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. **Number of single-element subsets.**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    For a multiset containing 3 elements, there are:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \binom{3}{1} = 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    (choose 1 element to be in the subset and all other elements will be in the empty set.)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. **Counting the number of non-empty subsets.**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    The total number of subsets has 128 elements, and 3 are single-element subsets. Therefore, the number of non-empty subsets is:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    128 - 3 = 125
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Putting these together, the number of different combinations of non-empty subsets from a set of 7 elements is:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \boxed{125}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Classically, one can consider \(A\) as \(U({\mathbb R}^{n},\mathcal M_n)\) where \(U({\mathbb R}^{n},\mathcal M_n)\) denotes the class of all linear maps on \({\mathbb R}^{n}\) and \(\mathcal M_n\) the algebra of the matrices, together with the restriction \(U(A) \subseteq A(\mathbb R^n)\) being an injective coequalizer (composed only of identity functors). The question is then the simplest formulation of the characteristic classes.
