[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For any finitely generated abelian group $R$ and any ring $R_{\text{alg}}$ it is meaningless to discuss whether $R_{\text{alg}}$ is an $R$-algebra. Indeed, according to the "traditional" definition of an algebraic structure you can add a definition of $R$-algebra. However, one particularly useful definition has been made known, namely:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m (A commutative ring in a (not necessarily finite) neval domain ) is called a Banach algebra if it has the following properties:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m A Banach algebra $R$ is called associative iff for all $a,b,c\in R$ with $a\circ b\circ c=b\circ c$, it follows that $\circ$ is an associative binary operation.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The first definition remarks exactly your first statement:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $(R_{\text{alg}},\circ)$ is a Banach algebra
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m where $(R_{\text{alg}},\circ)$ is a commutative ring. That is any ring $(R_{\text{alg}},\circ)$ is a Banach algebra.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The second definition is equivalent to your second one. However, most authors recognize the first one. The related definition you asked about is the second one (for rings).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m And for finite neval free algebras, the first is equivalent to your third statement:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Any finite neval free sugal algebra $R$ is a Banach algebra
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The third statement merely allocates the terminology to the first. However, that first might have been used, since it refers to the weak convergence in both normal and non-normal theorem of Brown.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m However, about the equivalence right comment also mentioned, you can find essentially the first definition in
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m "It is somewhat remarkable that Banach algebras generally hold the same affirmations about their non-normal algebras as weakly continuous algebras do about their continuous ones (since the Banach-algebra condition is stronger than weak-order continuity, and furthermore all (compact) automorphism groups of the non-normal algebras are trivial from this point of view, they behave just as those of the simple and simple-Algebras do)" - Ratiu
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
