[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Your question sparked an interesting discussion about Banach algebras and their properties, and the equivalence relation established between non-normal algebras and Banach algebras. Let's delve into the details and clarify the statements you made.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### Equivalence Relation in Banach Algebras
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m You noted the equivalence between a Banach algebra \( (R_{\text{alg}}, \circ) \) and a Banach space \( (R, \cdot) \) on page 4 of the Eilenberg-MacLane Group Theory and Functional Analysis I lecture notes. This equivalence highlights the fact that both \( (R_{\text{alg}}, \circ) \) and \( (R, \cdot) \) are Banach categories, and the uniqueness of arbitrary (Afriedrich-Zurich) extension results modulo certain equivalence classes of Banach algebras (B-Algebras). The equivalence relation is quite complex and symmetric, ensuring that every Banach algebra is of the form \( (R_{\text{alg}}, \circ) \), but some B-Algebras do not match entirely due to the non-uniqueness of homomorphisms.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### The equivalence is defined by fiat
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Merely saying ("the equivalence relation established between Banach algebras and Banach spaces is defined by fiat") is incorrect; it should be left as a defining axiom.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### The equivalence relies on the commutativity of \( R_{\text{alg}} \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The conventional construction of a Banach algebra involves the existence of a commutative ring \( R_{\text{alg}} \) and an associative operation \( \circ \). For any ring \( R \), if \( R_{\text{alg}} \) is commutative, then naturally defined "arithmetic\" processes can be regarded as functions \( R \) × \( R \) → \( R_{\text{alg}} \) (acts on composites), thus forming a ring in the context of \( R_{\text{alg}} \). This definition implicitly relies on the commutativity of \( R_{\text{alg}} \) to establish the algebraic structure.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### The equivalence includes standard cases
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The equivalence includes examples encountered in finite neval free algebras. For example:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( \mathbb R [x] \cong \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb R[x]/(x^2-\cdots)) \) as Banach algebras.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( \mathbb Q [x] \cong \lim_{n \to \infty} \mathbb Q[x - a]/(x^n -p_n) \) as Banach algebras.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( \mathbb Q [j
