[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This requires converting the polar coordinates to rectangular coordinates. Go from the polar coordinates ${\theta} = {\frac{\pi}{3}}$ to rectangular coordinates ${\vec{P}_1}$. We need to find $x$ and $y$ coordinates. $$ \left\{{\theta} = {\frac{\pi}{3}}\right\}={2 \sqrt{3}}=\sqrt{3}\cdot2.$$ $$x = {\sqrt{3}}\cdot2\cos\frac{\pi}{3}=\sqrt{3}\cdot2\cdot\frac{1}{2}=\sqrt{3}.$$ $$y = {\sqrt{3}}\cdot2\sin\frac{\pi}{3}=\sqrt{3}\cdot2\cdot\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=\sqrt{3}\cdot2\cdot\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}=3.$$ thus thereby $$ x = -3, \ y = 3.$$
