[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If $\theta$ is the angle between the diagonals, then find $\cos \theta.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let $D$ be the foot of the altitude of the parallelogram $ABCD.$ Then $AD$ bisects $\angle ABC.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\angle A + \angle B = 180^\circ$ and $\angle D + \angle C = 180^\circ.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since every side of the opposed parallelogram is parallel to the $x$ and $y$ axis, and every diagonal is at a $45^\circ $ angle, we can calculate the area of $\\triangle ACD$ and the area of $\\triangle BCD$ as $\frac12 \cdot ED \cdot CB$ and $\frac12 ED \cdot OD$ respectively.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now we can calculate $\cos \angle DCA.$ Since the area of $\\triangle DCA$ is $\frac12 MD \cdot DC$ we have that $MC = \frac MD \cdot DC = ED \cdot CB.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Similarly, a calculation tells us that $OD = \frac 12 ED \cdot DC.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore $\cos \theta = \frac{MC \cdot OD}{AC} = \frac{ED \cdot CB \cdot OD}{AC}.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Computing gives $\cos \theta = \frac -4 \sqrt{29} - -1 \div \sqrt{49 + \frac72 \cdot 4 \cdot 1}\cdot \sqrt{29 \cdot \frac{1 + 2 \cdot 1}{\left(1 + 2 \cdot 1\right)^2}}.$. Then $\cos \theta = \frac72 \sqrt{10}.$
