[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's start by generating all possible values for \(m\) and finding corresponding \(n\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Initialize a variable to count how many pairs \((m,n)\) satisfy the inequality.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Loop through all possible integer values of \(m\) from 1 to \(\lfloor \sqrt{22} \rfloor = 5\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. For each value of \(m\), loop through all possible integer values of \(n\) from 1 to \(\lfloor \sqrt{22 - m^2} \rfloor + 1 = 4\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 4. Check if the inequality \(m^2 + n < 22\) is satisfied for each pair \((m,n)\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
