[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To solve the problem, we need to understand the relationship between exponential and rational exponents. The expression involves multiplying terms with the same base but different exponents. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Given: $$(10^{0.5})(10^{0.3})(10^{0.2})(10^{0.1})(10^{0.9})$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We can simplify this using the property of logarithms. The property is:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$(a \times b^c) = a^c \times b^{c^2}$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In this case, the bases are $10$ and the exponents are the same. Since there are 6 terms, the product inside the exponent simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$10^{0.5 \times 6} = 10^{3} = 1000$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, the value of $$$(10^{0.5})(10^{0.3})(10^{0.2})(10^{0.1})(10^{0.9})$$$$ is $$1000$$
