[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Yes, that's correct. We first find out the least and greatest two-digit multiple of 13, which are 42 and 55, respectively.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If $x$ is the greatest two-digit multiple of 13, then we know $13x$ is a multiple of 13 and a two-digit number.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since $x=42$ and $55=15\cdot3$, we have $42\cdot14$ as a multiple of 13 and $45\cdot18$ as a multiple of 13. So, the greatest two-digit multiple of 13, according to the problem, is 45.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Similarly, if $x$ is the least two-digit multiple of 13, then we know $-13x$ is a multiple of 13 and a two-digit number.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since $x=-45$ and $-48=16\cdot3$, we have $-45\cdot16$ as a multiple of 13 and $-39\cdot16$ as a multiple of 13. So, the least two-digit multiple of 13, according to the problem, is 25.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Adding 45 and 25 together, we get $M + N = 70$. Therefore, the value of $M + N$ is $\boxed{70}$.酏ingo<|endoftext|>
