[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The graphs of $y=b$ and $y+c$ intersect at exactly one point if and only if $c$ is the unique value that makes the distance between the points $-b,-c$ on the $y$-axis equal to the distance between the points $a,-a$ on the $x$-axis. The distance between the points $x=a$ and $x=c$ is therefore $\sqrt{a-c}$. Drawing a picture, we know that this occurs when $a=c+1$, so the result is $\boxed{3}$ inch.
