[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Acme T-Shirt Company charges a $\$50$ set-up fee plus $\$9$ for  each shirt printed. Beta T-shirt Company has no set up fee, but charges $\$14$ per shirt. What is the minimum number of shirts for which a customer saves money by using Acme?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the minimum number of shirts for which a customer saves money by using Acme, we can calculate the total cost from both companies for each additional shirt.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If a customer prints N shirts, there are N + 1 shirts in total. Using Acme, the total cost is \$9N + \$50, and using Beta, the total cost is \$(14N).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We want to find the number of shirts (N) for which using Beta saves more money than using Acme. Therefore, we set up the inequality:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 14N - 9N &= 5N \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 14N - ((14N - 9N)) &= 5N \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 14N - (5N) &= 9N \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 9N &= 5N \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since 9N is not equal to 5N, the customer would incur more cost using Acme. This shows that there is no minimum number of shirts for which a customer saves money by using Acme. Thus, we say that it is impossible to find a specific minimal number of shirts.<|endoftext|>
