[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Consider the graph of the function \( y = a \sin bx \). The period of this function is given by the value of \( \frac{2\pi}{b} \), since the period of the sine function \( \sin(x) \) is \( 2\pi \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In this problem, the period of \( y = a \sin bx \) is \( \frac{2\pi}{b} \). From the graph, we can identify the period, but it's not directly useful since the y-coordinate (either 1 or -1) of the graph indicates a specific period. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's focus on finding \( b \) and \( a \) by relating the height of the average value of \( y = a \sin bx \) to the given x-values.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The average value of a function \( f(x) \) over an interval \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \text{Average value} = \frac{1}{x_{1} - x_{0}} \left[ f(x_0) + f(x_1) + \cdots + f(x_{n-1}) \right]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For \( y = a \sin bx \), at a typical instance \( x = x_1 \), the average value can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \text{Average value} = \frac{1}{x_1 - x_0} \left[ a \sin b (x_1) + \cdots + (-1) \sin b (x_{n-1}) \right]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Evaluating at \( x = x_i \), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m a = 1 \quad \text{and} \quad \sin b = -1 \Rightarrow \cos b = -1 \Rightarrow b = 2\pi
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, substituting \( a = 1 \) and \( b = 2\pi \) into the average value formula gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \text{Average value} = \frac{1}{(x_1 - x_0)} \left[ \sin (2\pi x_1) + \cdots + (-1) \sin (2\pi x_{n-1}) \right]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The values for the other function evaluations \( \sin (2\pi x) \) at \( x_i \) are:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - At \( x = 0 \), \( \sin y_0 = 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - At \( x = 1 \), \( \sin y_1 = \sin \pi = 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - At \( x = 2 \), \( \sin y_2 = \sin 2\pi = 0 \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m At these, the corresponding \( a \) values are:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( a = 1 \) for \( a \) being 1
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - \( a = 1 \) for \( a \) being 1
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Both give the same sum of negative term contributions \( -1 \). An average downward slope point is therefore significant.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Usually, the deviation from zero divided by the period effectively amounts to a deviation of the actual function \( y = a \sin bx \) from the averaged sinusoidal model.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, given sufficient sample data, particularly good values of \( a \) and \( b \):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Linear regression calculations:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Compute \( b \in \boxed{\frac{2\pi}{x_0}} \) based on average \( \frac{1}{x_0 - x_2} \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
