[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Given the function $y = \frac{x+2}{5x-7}$, where it always approaches infinity as $x$ approaches 2, but never actually equals $2$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, if we look://.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If $x=\frac{x+2}{5x-7}$,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m we could deduce that $x = \frac{x+2}{5x-7}-\frac{x}{5x-7} = \frac{(x+2)-(x)}{5x-7} = \frac{2}{5x-7}$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The denominator obviously can't equal $0$, in which case there would have to be infinity caused by a division going in the denominator on the left of the equality sign.Also, a division cannot equal a non-zero value unless the divisor is 0.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore we have to take $0$ as the division constant, which gives $x = \frac{2}{5x-7}$, which when we rearrange gives $5x-7 = 2 \to x = \frac{9}{5}=\boxed{1.8}$.
