[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 	$$\sqrt[3]{4y^2} = 4$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m (Our initial assumption was that $y = x^2$, so we can replace $y$ with $x^2$.) Reducing the exponent couldn't hurt, so we'll do:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$\sqrt[3]{4} \cdot \sqrt[3]{y^2} = 4$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since the cube root of $x^2$ is $y$, which can be approximated as the square root of $x^2$, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$\sqrt[3]{4} \cdot \sqrt[3]{y^2} = \sqrt[3]{4} \cdot y$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This gives us a single relationship:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$y^{2/3} = y$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$y^{1/3} = 1$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since $y$ is a non-negative number and the only real solution to this equation is $y = 1$, we find that $x^2 = 1$:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$ x^2 = 1$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the only solution for $x$ is $x = \boxed{1}$.
