[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m A row of 8 light switches controls different banks of lights in a school gymnasium. In preparation for a school dance, students decide that they want either one or two banks of lights on during the dance. How many possible ways are there of setting these 8 switches so that either one or two banks of lights are on?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to set the 8 switch settings such that either one or two banks of lights are on during the school dance. We have 3 Bank switches and 4 Light switches, so we can express the total number of switch setups as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \(3x + 4y\), where \(x\) is the number of setups where one bank is on, and \(y\) is the number of setups where no bank is on.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - Each \(x\) represents the case of one bank being on.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - Each \(y\) represents the case where the other \(3 - x\) banks are not on.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For the sum to be even, there are 2 possible scenarios for \(x\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Both \(x\) and \(y\) are even.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Both \(x\) and \(y\) are odd.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since none of which pair shall give us 0 setups, \(x\) and \(y\) must have opposite parity.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore,:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - For even \(x\), \(y\) must be odd.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - For odd \(x\), \(y\) must be even.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m There are 4 choices for \(x\) (even), and 4 choices for 1 odd and 4 choices for 1 even, giving a total of \(4(4) = 16\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Meanwhile, there are 16 choices for \(y\), and 4 choices for \(x\) (even), contributing \(4 \times 16 = 64\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Finally, multiply the number of setups only when \(x\) is even by the total setups \(= 4 \times 64 = 256\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\boxed{256}\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m untosnout(x)<|endoftext|>
