[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m A dilation centered at the origin takes $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix}$ to $\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ -21 \end{pmatrix}.$  Which vector does the dilation take $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}$ to?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m With an unknown vector $α$ we have the mapping $f: \mathbb{R}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^2$ given by $f\left( \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} \right) = \begin{pmatrix} ax + by \\ cx + dy \end{pmatrix},$ where
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix} = f\left( \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ 7 \end{pmatrix} \right) = \begin{pmatrix} -12 \\ -21 \end{pmatrix} \text{ and }\begin{pmatrix} c \\ d \end{pmatrix} = f\left( \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \right) = \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Comparing this to the equation (or solution, you will have to choose a frame of reference in which the vector is uniquely determined),
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m we see that
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m a & = -12, \bf b & = -21- 3 = -24, \qquad c & = - 24, \qquad d & = - 2.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Hence $f\left( \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} \right)$ produces
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$\begin{pmatrix} -12 & -24 \\ -24 & -2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 14 \\ -11 \end{pmatrix}.$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\blacksquare$<|endoftext|>
