[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m and 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m e^(i beta) = cos(beta) + i sin(beta).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, to find $\sin (\alpha + \beta)$, we can use the trigonometric identities:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m sin(alpha + beta) = sin(alpha)cos(beta) + cos(alpha)sin(beta),
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m cos(alpha + beta) = cos(alpha)cos(beta) - sin(alpha)sin(beta).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We also know that:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m sin^2(alpha) + cos^2(alpha) = 1
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m and
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m sin^2(beta) + cos^2(beta) = 1.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Using these identities, we can substitute the values of cos(alpha) and sin(alpha). For e^(i alpha):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m cos(alpha) = (e^(i alpha) + e^(-i alpha)) / 2,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m sin(alpha) = (e^(i alpha) - e^(-i alpha)) / 2.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For e^(i beta):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m cos(beta) = (e^(i beta) + e^(-i beta)) / 2,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m sin(beta) = (e^(i beta) - e^(-i beta)) / 2.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, to find $\sin (\alpha + \beta)$, we can substitute the above identities into the given equation:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
