[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In this cube, we now have left four edges of the same length, and we need to choose three of them to traverse. For each of the $3!$ possible permutations of the edges, we can choose any one of the remaining two edges from those six edges. Therefore, there are $\displaystyle \binom{6}{3}\cdot 3!$ possible permutations. 
