[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Given \(\left|x+2y-4\right|+\left|x-1\right|+\left|3-x\right|=0\), find \(x^{y}\). To solve the equation \(\left|x+2y-4\right| + \left|x-1\right| + \left|3-x\right| = 0\), we need to understand that the sum of absolute values of a set of numbers is zero if and only if each individual number is zero. This is because the absolute value function \(|a|\) is always non-negative, meaning that the sum of non-negative numbers can only be zero if each of the numbers is individually zero.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Solve for \(x\) and \(y\) from the equation \(\left|x+2y-4\right| + \left|x-1\right| + \left|3-x\right| = 0\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
