[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let $\mathbf{M}$ be a matrix, and let $\mathbf{v}$ and $\mathbf{w}$ be vectors, such that
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\mathbf{M} \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} \quad \text{and} \quad \mathbf{M} \mathbf{w} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}.\]Compute $\mathbf{M} (-2 \mathbf{v} + \mathbf{w}).$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To compute \\(\mathbf{M}((-2 \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w}))\\), we can use the distributive property of matrix multiplication over scalar multiplication. According to this rule, the matrix multiplication \\(\mathbf{M}((-2 \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w}))\\) can be rewritten as:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \\(\mathbf{M}((-2 \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w}))*\\)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now we apply the outer product rule, which states that \\(\mathbf{M}a = a\mathbf{M}^T\\) for any square matrix \\(\mathbf{M}\\) and any vectors \\(a\\) and its transpose. Therefore, we can reduce the matrix multiplication \\((-2 \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w})\\) to a single vector, which we will denote as \\(\mathbf{v}\\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \\(\mathbf{M}((-2 \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w})) = (-2 \mathbf{v}+\mathbf{w}) = \mathbf{v} + 2\mathbf{w}\\)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So we have the final result: \\(\boxed{{\mathbf{v}+2\mathbf{w}}}\\).
