[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ 3 \cdot |\triangle|^3 = 4 \cdot 2^k \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Assuming \(\triangle\) is the leading digit of 3, let's try \(\triangle = 2\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ 3 \cdot |2|^3 = 4 \cdot 2^k \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ 3 \cdot 8 = 4 \cdot 10^k \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ 24 = 40 \cdot 10^k \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ 6 = \cdot 10^k \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ k = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This is consistent, as 6 can be represented as \(2 + 1 = 22\) in base 10. Thus, \(\triangle = 2\) is a valid solution. But let's check if there are other possible values for \(\triangle\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's check if \(\triangle = 3\) would also work:
