[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the sum of the rational roots of $g(x) = x^3 - 9x^2 + 16x - 4$, we first need to determine the rational roots of the polynomial. The Rational Root Theorem states that if $p(q)$ is a root of a polynomial $p(x) = q(x)$ with integer coefficients, then $p(q)$ is a root as well. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m For this polynomial, the denominator (x-1) divides the polynomial entirely, so the roots are also factors of the constant term and the leading coefficient:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$x = -1$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This is because the constant term is -4 and its factorization over the rational numbers is already done in the Rational Root Theorem. Also, the first factor of the leading coefficient is 1, so multiplying by -1 will not produce any other roots during the rational root check. Thus, the rational roots of $g(x)$ are:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$x = -1$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, to find the sum of the rational roots, we sum them:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$-\frac{1}{1}$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, adding these values gives us:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$-1$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\boxed{-1}$<|endoftext|>
