[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2) $x - y = 2t - 2$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3) The point of intersection of these lines
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Our goal is to find the slope of the line that has as its points of intersection those points on the lines. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Note that 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$\begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ay + bx + c &= d\\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m in this case, we see that $ad = bd + ac \implies 3d + b(-1) = 24, which implies b = 6\end{align}$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m so 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$\begin{align}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ax + 2y + 6 = 7t + 3 \implies a(7t + 3) + 2y + 6 = 7[7t + 3] + 3 \implies 38t + 9 = 59t + 21 \implies 22 = 21t \implies t = \frac{22}{21}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align}$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m and plotting will give the line $29x + 63y = 77$ on $x = t$, below
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ![screenshot of graphing program with points marked and a line on t-axis.](https://i.stack.imgur.com/jH5v4.png)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since on top of by '= 77', we have the constant, we will get the slope since it is a constant coefficient.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The slope would be $\left( \frac{63}{29} \right)^2 - \left( \frac{22}{21} \right) \cdot {-18} = \frac{6470}{8539} = \boxed{\frac{1298}{1807}}$.<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
