[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m What is the last digit of the base $6$ representation of the base ten integer $355$?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To determine the last digit of the base $6$ representation of the base $10$ integer $355$, we will follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Convert the number $355$ from base $10$ to base $6$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Identify the last digit in base $6$ representation.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### Step 1: Convert from Base $10$ to Base $6$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To convert $355$ from base $10$ to base $6$, we perform successive division by $6$ and keep track of the remainders:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{array}{r}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 6 \div 355 = 0 \quad \text{(remainder is } 3\text{)} \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 355 \div 6 = 59 \quad \text{(remainder is } 1\text{)}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{array}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the base $10$ representation of $355$ in base $6$ is $\dots 11_6$, where the positions represent $6^3$, $6^2$, and $6^1$ respectively, and we usually start with the lowest power. We have already reached $3$ higher than $6^2$, so we disregard the $6^2$ part and put the remainder $59$ down to the right of the $11$ part of this quotient.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the base $6$ representation of $355$ is $\boxed{5911}_6$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ### Step 2: Identify the Last Digit
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, we look at the digits in the last digit: $5$, $9$, and $1$. Among the digits $0$ to $9$, the last digit is always $0$ or $5$. If we were to apply this logic to any number, we would note that the last digit is commonly identified or processed as $5$ depending on context, but in this case, since $355$ is an odd number, the last digit is not $5$ but $1$.
