[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To convert from spherical coordinates \((\rho, \theta, \phi)\) to rectangular (Cartesian) coordinates \((x, y, z)\), we need to use the following formulas:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m x &= \rho \sin \phi \cos \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m y &= \rho \sin \phi \sin \theta \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m z &= \rho \cos \phi
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{aligned}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Given the spherical coordinates \((\rho, \theta, \phi) = \left( 3, \frac{5 \pi}{12}, 0 \right)\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. **Convert the angle \(\phi\):**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - \(\phi = 0\) is the polar angle, which corresponds to \(0^\circ\) or \(0\) radians.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. **Substitute \(\phi = 0\) into the equations:**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Replace \(\phi\) with 0 in the equations:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      x = \rho \sin 0 \cos \theta = 0 \cdot \sin \theta = 0
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      y = \rho \sin 0 \sin \theta = 0 \cdot \sin \theta = 0
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      z = \rho \cos 0 = 3 \cdot 1 = 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m      \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. **Conclusion:**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - The rectangular coordinates \((x, y, z)\) corresponding to the spherical coordinates \((\rho, \theta, \phi) = \left( 3, \frac{5 \pi}{12}, 0 \right)\) are \((0, 0, 3)\).
