[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Find the remainder when the polynomial $x^{1000}$ is divided by the polynomial $(x^2 + 1)(x + 1).$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the remainder when $x^{1000}$ is divided by $(x^2 + 1)(x + 1)$, we will use polynomial long division. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 1: Set up the polynomials in a long division form:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \frac{x^{1000}}{x^2 + 1}(x + 1)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 2: Divide the leading term of the dividend by the leading term of the divisor:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The leading term of the dividend is $x^{1000}$ and the leading term of the divisor is $x^2$. So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$\frac{x^{1000}}{x^2} = x^{998}$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 3: Multiply the divisor by this term and subtract it from the dividend:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m & x^{1000} - x^{998} \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m & \quad x^2(x + 1) + x^{998} \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \hline
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 0 & x^{1000} \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Step 4: The remainder is the result from Step 3 (excluding the last term $x^{998}$):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$0 ~x^{998}$$
