[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m t = -\frac{1}{3}, \quad u = -\frac{4}{3}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m These values describe a line that passes through the point \((-3, 2)\) and \(u = -\frac{4}{3}\). We can determine the direction vector of the line:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 2 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} -3 \cdot -\frac{4}{3} \\ 2 \cdot -\frac{4}{3} \\ -10 \cdot -\frac{4}{3} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 2 \\ -10 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -\frac{8}{3} \\ \frac{40}{3} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -7 \\ \frac{4}{3} \\ -\frac{106}{3} \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The direction vector is also known as the slope of the line, which is \(-\frac{5}{7}\). The line passes through \((-3, 2)\), so its slope is \(-\frac{2}{3}\). The angle \(\theta\) between the two lines is related to the slope by the formula \(\tan(2\theta) = -\frac{1}{\tan(\theta)}\). We know the angle between the lines is \(\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)\) because the angle is obtuse and the line slopes downward.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We can use the double-angle identity \(\sin(2\theta) = 2\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta)\) to find \(\sin(2\theta)\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \sin(2\theta) = 2 \cdot \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2}} = 2 \cdot \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{\sqrt{\frac{13}{9}}} = \frac{4}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{\frac{\sqrt{13}}{3}} = \frac{4}{\sqrt{13}}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Now, we find \(\cos(2\theta)\):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \cos(2\theta) = \cos^2(\theta) - \sin^2(\theta) = \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 - \left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2 = 0
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Since \(\cos(2\theta) = 0\) when \(\theta = 90^\circ\) (or \(\frac{\pi}{2}\) radians), the angle \(\theta\) must be obtuse. Therefore, \(\theta = 180^\circ - \frac{\pi}{2} = 157.5^\circ\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The acute angle formed by the two lines at \(P\) is \(\boxed{90}\) degrees.<|endoftext|>
