[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m (Activity) 2 days BME has gone to November. The others have gone to December 30th.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Note: Answer does NOT include trips around Ventana.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We can prove that at least one person will go to the chess club.  This process runs for four days, such that every person who goes to the chess club on day $i$ simultaneously guards the fifth day by the corresponding choice, $j|i$.  The choices are limited by the fact that it must be the same order.  Thus, we have four people $(i,j)$, with $1<<j<<i$ so none of them can make a transition to the chess club on day $i$.  Thus, at least one person will go to the chess club on November 30$$.  
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Maybe suppose by mistake that it was the case that $n$ of these $$i,j$$ pairs are selected together.  (In an event of this likelihood, $i\in2$ and $n\equiv2(3)$, and so on.)  The probability that $i$ does not take up the last position is $(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)$.  If we are masked and resorted this using one person from each of the three colours, we can work out that the expected value of $n$ is ${737 \over 180}$ days.  Thus the minimum number of days for a person to go to chess garcia is $4 n$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Second fastest way is probably $(1 -8n^2, 97 \over {882 \over 399n-1588n^2})$, which gives the exact same answer.  I don't have the code for $$\over{2n^2 + 2n + U}\ {m-\left<U\over9\over 50\over 348\right}.}$$ here, but I worked out the answer by one of my collgents Brian Gladwell.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m @hidden: The problem is solving bipartite matching/partition. One can use maximum cuts to get the integer part of the answer for all the limit cases. Otherwise, it can just be $2$ in every case.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's denote this as a bipartite graph, with colour $1$ representing node A and colour $2$ node B. Each vertex will have counter-clockwise colour or counter-clockwise adjacent points, forming a sequence noted by $C_{nr}$ where $n$ is the number of points on the edge and $r$ the colour of that edge. However, the sum of the $C_{nr}$ of every $n$ is $0$, as every node in the anticore's neighbourhood must have two (if not four) edges, and in each bloc, every node (one of the edges or of a node on the middle of a edge) have only edges to the left or to the right, respectively. This is obvious since giving $2n$ edges attaches $n$ of them to the two countries, each with $2$ edges left over.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let $A$ (and correspondingly $B$, and $B$) be the number of edges between two synchrotron stations, and let the number of edges between any two countries be represented by $S$. As the graph is bipartite: if $S_1 >S_2 >S_3 \dots S_n >S$, then either $A_i=S_i$, $B = S_i$ for some $i$, or there are $A_i = B_i$ for all $i $, and $S_1 >S_2 >S_3\dots$. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m It can be concluded that if you want to minimise the sum of $1$ to the edges between the two stations, then you want the $i$-th colour to have a difference of  $\left(\frac{n}{\gcd(\lceil S_{i+1},S\rceil,\lceil S_{i+2}\rceil) -\lceil S_{i}\rceil}{\mu(L_i\times S_{i+1})}-1\right)$ greater than $L_i\times L_{i+1}$/$\mu(L_{i +1} \times S_{i +2})$, that means the better value for a coloured $i$-th edge it to have a max count $>n^*$ such that $n^* = L_i\times L_{i+1}$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m This can easily bounded by the twin-prime $e$ prime sequence $e =\{2\cdotodd\cdot 3,\cdot\cdot,2\cdotodd\cdot 2\cdot 3\cdot 1,\cdot\cdot\}$, where the $i$-th prime of the $n / k$-th pair is its maximum prime.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\lim\limits\limits_{nth} = \frac{1}{\pi^2}$ is better than $\frac{3}{12} 2$, and so these two areas asymptote to each-other.<|endoftext|>
