[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Betty has between $40$ and $50$ pennies that she wants to arrange in a rectangular array. She notices she can arrange the pennies in three different ways without any gaps or spaces. However, if Betty adds two more pennies to her collection, she can only arrange the pennies in one way without any gaps or spaces: a straight line. How many pennies did Betty originally have?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Note: An $a \times b$ rectangle is considered the same as a $b \times a$ rectangle.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let $n$ be the number of pennies Betty originally has. We know that $n \in \{40, 41, 42, 43,\ldots ,50\}$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m When Betty adds two more pennies, she can create a $n \times n$ rectangle. For this to be the case, either $n$ is even, or $n$ is not divisible by two and there are no other possibilities for the number of pennies Betty has.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, we only need to check the possibilities for $n$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. If $n$ is even, it is possible for Betty to have any amount of pennies between 40 and 50. Now, $n-1$ pennies can also be arranged in a $n \times n$ rectangle of the same form. So, in this case, Betty has $n$ pennies, $n-1$ pennies, and another $1$ penny.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. If $n$ is not divisible by two and there are no other possibilities, it is also possible for Betty to have any amount of pennies between 40 and 50. Now, $n-2$ pennies can also be arranged in a $n \times n$ rectangle of the same form. So, in this case, Betty has $n$ pennies, $n-2$ pennies, and another $2$ pennies.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the possible number of pennies Betty originally has is any subset of $\{40, 41, 42, 43,\ldots ,50\}$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, the final answer is any number between 40 and 50, inclusive.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ####
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Betty originally has between 40 and 50 pennies.<|endoftext|>
