[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m My usual workout consists of running 10 laps around a 400 meter track. If I go to a track that is only 250 meters long, how many laps will I need to run to get the same workout?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To determine how many laps you need to run to complete the same workout, you'll follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. **Calculate the relationship between the distances:**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Distance to 10 laps around a 400-meter track: \( 400 \text{ meters/lap} \times 10 \text{ laps} = 4000 \text{ meters} \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Distance to a 250-meter track: \( 250 \text{ meters/lap} \times 1 \text{ lap} = 250 \text{ meters} \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. **Determine the number of laps needed to get the same distance:**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - To get the same distance back-to-back, you need two laps: \( 4000 \text{ meters} / 2 = 2000 \text{ meters} \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. **Utility of the 250-meter track:**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Even though you only want two laps, it's beneficial to account for any round-trip considerations. Consider the total distance: \( 4000 \text{ meters} + 250 \text{ meters} = 4250 \text{ meters} \)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 4. **Decision on whether to run on the original or shortened track:**
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Since 4000 meters (4 laps) would require 2000 meters (timer total), it fits easier on a 250-meter track because it directly relates to running a shorter distance.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Running a full 10 laps around a 250-meter track using a 400-meter track would be equivalent to making the same total distance (4250 meters) on the 400-meter track.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - To complete both sets of laps, 4000 meters are needed, and since it guides the distance calculation, running on a 400-meter track is a more advantageous approach.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, running 10 laps around a 400-meter track to get the same workout would help maintain efficiency when running on a 250-meter track comfortably. \(\boxed{\boxed{10}}\)<|endoftext|>
