[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In how many different ways can four students stand in a straight line if two of the students refuse to stand next to each other?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We can create two different cases.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Case 1: Case a stands and Case b stands on the right side.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Case a: There are 3 ways to arrange Student 1, 2 and 3, because the restricted student (Case a) has to stand on the left side, so we have to follow the permutation formula. Think of the students as a permutation problem.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Case b stands and Case a stands on the left side.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Case b: There are 3 ways to arrange Student 1, 2 and 3, since there are no restrictions on the students.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, there are a total of $3 + 3 = 6$ unique ways to arrange four students in a line, so the answer is: $\boxed{6}$<|endoftext|>
