[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Find the remainder when $x^3 - 3x + 5$ is divided by $x + 2.$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We want to find the remainder when $P(x) = x^{3} - 3x + 5$ is divided by $Q(x) = x + 2$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m According to the Remainder Theorem, the remainder of a polynomial division by a linear polynomial $x - a$ is equal to $P(a)$. In this case, $a = -2$, so we substitute $-2$ for $x$ in the expression $x^{3} - 3x + 5$ to find the remainder.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\Longrightarrow P(-2) = (-2)^{3} - 3(-2) + 5$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $\Longrightarrow P(-2) = -8 + 6 + 5$
