[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Find $|10-13i|\cdot |10+13i|$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Background
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The calculation of $|a+bi|\cdot |c+di|$ for complex numbers $a+bi$ and $c+di$ is known as the Geometry of Complex Numbers. Here, let's focus on $10-13i$ and $10+13i$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's convert the complex numbers to their rectangular (Cartesian) form for easier calculation. Complex numbers are written in rectangular form as $x + yi$, where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers. We will require two axes here.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m (10 - 13i)&=(10)x - 13y\\[0.3 cm]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m (10 + 13i)&= (10)x + 13y
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Then we can see, the cross product $|10-13i|$ is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$|10-13i| =  \sqrt{(10x)^2 + (-13y)^2} = \sqrt{100x^2 + 169y^2}$$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m |(10 + 13i)| &=  \sqrt{ (10x)^2 + (13y)^2} = \sqrt{ 100x^2 + 169y^2} = (10x+13y) \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m (10-13i)(10+13i) &= (10x)^2 - (13y)^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m &=  100x^2 + 169y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m =& |10-13i|^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m =& (10x+13y)^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m =& 10^2x^2 + 2 \cdot 10x \cdot 13y + 13^2 y^2 \\
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m =& 100x^2 + 260xy + 169y^2
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m According to the formula, now we have
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m |10-13i \cdot 10+13i| = \sqrt{100x^2+260xy+169y^2} = \sqrt{(|10-13i|^2)|10+13i|}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus we remember
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m |a-bi|^2 = a^2+b^2
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \begin{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m |10-13i \cdot 10+13i| = |10-13i|^2 = 10^2 + 13^2 = 169
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Parenting a few final words. Completing the proof of $|10-13i| = 10 \pm 13i$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m |10i-(13i)||10-(-13i)| = \sqrt{(0 \pm 1)^2 + 0^2} = \sqrt{(-1)^2} = 1
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m $$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m That completes the proof.<|endoftext|>
