[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the degrees of the smaller angle formed by the hands of a clock at 10 o'clock, we need to determine the position of the minute hand at this time.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Understand the relationship between minutes and hours on a clock face:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - 12 minutes is 1/12th of an hour.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Thus, each minute represents \( \frac{1}{12} \) of an hour.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Bring the minute hand to 10 o'clock by rotating it 10 full rotations.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - The complete rotation of a clock is \( 360^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Therefore, the 10 o'clock position is equivalent to \( 10 \times 10^\circ \mod 360^\circ \).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. Perform the calculation:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    10 \times 10 - 360^\circ = 100 - 360 = -260
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 4. Determine the equivalent positive angle modulo \( 360^\circ \):
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    -260 + 360 = 10
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the 10 o'clock position on a clock is 100 degrees relative to the 12 o'clock position. 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 5. Now we need to determine the position of the hour hand at 10 o'clock.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 6. Each hour on the clock represents \( \frac{1}{12} \) of an hour.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 7. Calculate the hour hand at 10 o'clock by finding the 10 o'clock position and converting it to an angle of 30 minutes (since 10 o'clock corresponds to 1/12 of an hour).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 8. The appropriate angle for 30 minutes at an angle of 10 o'clock:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Convert 10 o'clock to minutes:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    10 \text{ o'clock} \times \frac{1}{12} \text{ hour/minute} = \frac{10}{12} = \frac{5}{6} \text{ hours}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 9. Calculate the equivalent angle for 30 minutes at a \(\frac{5}{6}\) rotation from the 12 o'clock position:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Already have 360 - 100 = 260 degrees as we reduced the angles above.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    - Use the reduced steps:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    260 \div 10 = 26 \text{ full rotations of 35.66666666666667 degrees each.}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 10. So after subtracting the 2o'clock from 10o'clock, the remaining 4 rotations will be split:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Rotate the hour hand 4 full rotations (300 degrees):
