[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \end{align*} which clearly implies $3At=B$ and $2CAA+B=C$. The operation $9 \colon 2B \Rightarrow \tilde{9}=9+C$, so $\frac{9-C}{3A}=\tilde{9}+\frac{2B-C}{3}>(7-2C)(6-\tilde{A})$. Since the latter is positive, $7-C>6-\tilde{A}$. Say, I find a suitable value for the square $A + B$, so $B=6-\tilde{A}$ and $7-C=A+\frac{2B-C}{3}=(\left(A+
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \frac{1}{2}(A+B)\right)+\frac{1}{2}(A+B)-1).$ The case for the middle is even more restrictive, so I simplify the fractions $\frac{6-A}{1+A}(A-B)$. For constants that are multiples of 6, this is even harder to bring in controll, so I find the closest puddle yields $15$, $24$, $27$, $43$. For the middle looks too risky to actually show, it is easy to see that $24$ lies most likely in the first case. \addtocounter{page}{-1}\ref{merit1}<|endoftext|>
