[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m If $\det \mathbf{A} = 4$ and $\det \mathbf{B} = -5,$ then find $\det (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}).$
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find \(\det (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B})\), we can use the property of determinants which states that:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\det (\mathbf{ABC}) = \det (\mathbf{A}) \cdot \det (\mathbf{B}) \cdot \det (\mathbf{C}) \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \text{iff} \ \ \ \ \ \ \ (\mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}, \mathbf{C} \ \subset \ \mathbb{R}^3)\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m In this case, we have \(\mathbf{A} = \mathbf{B} = \mathbf{C} = \mathbf{I}\) (where \(\mathbf{I}\) is the identity matrix). Since we are working among real numbers, the identity matrix is also an irreducible matrix.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m That's because for any real number \(r\), \(rI\) is also an identity matrix, meaning \(rI = rI\) is always true for any real number \(r\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Therefore, \(\mathbf{I} \det (\mathbf{I}) = \det (\mathbf{I \times 1}) = \det (1) = 1\), regardless of the complex diagonal elements.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, \(\det (\mathbf{I} \times \mathbf{I \times 1}) = \det (\mathbf{1}) = 1\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Applying this general rule to our case:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[\det (\mathbf{A} \mathbf{B}) = \det (\mathbf{A}) \cdot \det (\mathbf{B}) = 4 \cdot (-5) = -20\]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the final answer is \(\boxed{-20}\).<|endoftext|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
