[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m The lengths in feet of three pieces of timber are 48, 72, and 40. The sawmill operator needs to cut the timber into logs of equal length with no waste. How many feet long is the greatest possible length she can cut?
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the greatest possible length of the timber that the sawmill operations will allow the operators to cut, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the lengths 48, 72, and 40. The GCD is the largest positive integer that divides each of the given lengths without leaving a remainder.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, we find the prime factorization of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 48 = 2^4 \times 3
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 72 = 2^3 \times 3^2
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 40 = 2^3 \times 5
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Next, we determine the GCD by taking the lowest power of each prime that appears in all three factorizations:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \text{GCD} = 2^3 \times 3 = 8 \times 3 = 24
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the greatest possible length of the timber that the sawmill can cut, without any waste, is 24 feet.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
