[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, we will take the cosine of $1050^\circ$. We can use the identity $\cos(180^\circ + \theta) = -\cos \theta$ to simplify our calculation. By subtracting $90^\circ$ from $1050^\circ$, we can express it as $\cos 180^\circ + \cos(60^\circ)$.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \cos 180^\circ = -1
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \cos(60^\circ) = \frac{1}{2}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, $1050^\circ$ is $75^\circ$ more than an angle of $60^\circ$. For the cosine function to have the same sign in two adjacent angles, the cosine value of $180^\circ + \theta$ is $-1$. Therefore, $\cos 1050^\circ = -\cos 60^\circ = -\frac{1}{2}$.
