[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To figure out the number of ways to put 4 balls into 3 boxes without them being distinguishable, you need to figure out the possible combinations because some letters (balls) might be like another letter (box). For example, Mother would go into those of a different color, so that's how they fit into the equation!
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. The boxes will have 2 boxes with black marbles and 1 with white.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. These two groups (a mother or to present your daughter) will be considered identical and one of those will be taken (choosing the one most desirable). But the order doesn't matter.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, for each box, there are two supposed choices: either the mother is chosen or she's not chosen. Then mutliple choices can decide if your child can be chosen 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. We follow the 2nd rule (order doesn't matter) and multiply the possibilities inside the parentheses. That's what I said.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m {mother: 1 white, 1 black}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m {one: chosen white}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m {other: chosen black}
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m We multiply: 1*1*2 = 2.<|endoftext|>
