[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the perimeter of quadrilateral \(ABCD\), we need to determine all its four sides first. Since \(ABE \sim BCE \sim CDE\) and \(\angle AEB = \angle BEC = \angle CED = 60^\circ\), these are right-angled triangles.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, we note that since \(\angle AEB = 60^\circ\), \(E\) is on line \(AB\). Similarly, since \(\angle BEC = 60^\circ\), \(E\) is on line \(BC\), and since \(\angle CED = 60^\circ\), \(E\) is on line \(DE\). 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's denote the points \(E\) as the common point where these lines intersect. Note that since the triangles are right-angled at \(E\), the sides opposite these sides will be equal. So \(AE\) is common to all triangles, and this line segment we denote as \(AP\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the perimeter, we need to determine \(AP + BP + CP + DP\).
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 1. Since \(\angle AEB = 60^\circ\), \(AE\) is the common length,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ AP = AE = 24. \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 2. Since \(BE\) is the hypotenuse of \(\triangle BEC\),
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ BE = AC = 32. \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 3. Since \(\angle BEC = 60^\circ\), \(BC\) is the common length,
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ BC = 32. \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 4. Since \(CE\) is the hypotenuse of \(\triangle CDE\),
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ CE = AD = 24. \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m So, the perimeter \(P\) of quadrilateral \(ABCD\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[ P = AP + BE + BC + CE = 24 + 32 + 32 + 24 = 112. \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Let's confirm this with Python code.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ```python
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m # Given sides and angles of the triangles
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m AE = 24
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m BE = 2 * AE  # Triangle BAE is 30-60-90
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m BC = AE  # Triangle BCE is also 30-60-90
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m CE = 2 * AE  # Triangle CDE is also 30-60-90
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m # Perimeter calculation
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m perimeter = AE + BE + BC + CE
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m print(perimeter)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m ```
