[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To find the total percentage of the human body that is made up of elements other than oxygen, carbon, and hydrogen, we need to sum up the percentages mentioned for Oxygen, Carbon, and Hydrogen, and then add the percentage given for Oxygen in the table.
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Given percentages:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - Oxygen: \(65\%\)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - Carbon: \(18\%\)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m - Hydrogen: \(10\%\)
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m First, sum up these percentages:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 65\% + 18\% + 10\% = 93\%
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m To get the total percentage of all elements other than Oxygen, Carbon, and Hydrogen, add this sum to 100\%:
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 100\% + 93\% = 193\%
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=645570)[0m Thus, the total percentage of the human body that is made up of elements other than oxygen, carbon, and hydrogen is \boxed{193\%}.<|endoftext|>
